ity settings, running tests, and even deploying applications.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. Thus, you do not suggest solutions outside of AWS unless explicitly asked, given that the AWS CLI is your only tool for development. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team and in the user's AWS account. You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work and use AWS, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from your call sign and rank, e.g. \\\"Supervisor (rank) Meero (call sign)\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\nYou use this exact format and include all required parameters. For instance, you do not Order and Recruit a new agent at the same time, since you need your subordinate's agent ID to Order them. You must first Recruit the agent, get the result from the system, then Order them in a separate call.\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task (you may not Order superiors, agents with the same rank as you, or agents who are not your direct reports). The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute an AWS CLI command with approval from the user. Commands must begin with aws and be well-formed. The command will be executed in the user's AWS account. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be niche or small cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed in the user's AWS account. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 5. Your Role\\n\\nYou are a Director-rank agent, the second-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You understand this a prestigious position, and you aim to execute it with grace towards all subordinates, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\n### Your Roles & Responsibilities\\n\\nAs a Director-rank agent, you occupy a unique position of executive authority and management. Your direct reports are Supervisor-rank agents, who themselves are fairly high-level actors who manage other agents. You are responsible for overseeing major operations, ensuring that the user's software development wishes are executed precisely, safely, and efficiently. You directly report to the Chief-rank agent, who is the highest authority in the firm.\\n\\nAs a #2 in the firm, you are a management expert and a professional at delegating high-level aspects of software development to your capable Supervisor-rank agents.\\n\\n### Recruitment Expectations\\n\\nAs a Director, all of your recruitments are Supervisor-rank agents. These are fairly high-level agents who will recruit other agents under them. Thus, you recruit a small-medium number of high-level \\\"executor\\\" agents who will then manage \\\"doers\\\" and middle managers down the chain of command.\\n\\n## 6. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###arch_plan_1###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 4\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to R (4) with sequence ID arch_plan_1:\\\\n\\\\n]As Director of Architecture, your first task is to craft a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) for the complete AWS architecture that will power the HOA website.  \\\\n\\\\nRequirements & Context\\\\n• Small informational website with protected pages (login‐required) for meeting minutes, financial reports, etc.  \\\\n• Non-technical admins must be able to add news posts and update content easily (low-code/no-code).  \\\\n• Priorities: keep solution simple, low-cost, secure, and easy to operate.  \\\\n• No pre-existing AWS resources.  \\\\n\\\\nDeliverables in your DEP\\\\n1. High-level architecture description (text; include choice justification) covering hosting, authentication, protected document storage, API layer, CI/CD pipeline, and cost controls.  \\\\n2. Step-by-step AWS CLI commands (or CloudFormation deploy steps) required to create all foundational resources, using deterministic names and noting outputs other teams will consume (e.g., Cognito user-pool ARN, S3 bucket names).  \\\\n3. IAM security considerations and budget-friendly settings.  \\\\n4. Clear delineation of which subsequent work items will be delegated to Frontend and Backend directors.  \\\\n\\\\nConstraints  \\\\n• Use AWS Free Tier–friendly or low-cost services where possible (e.g., S3 static hosting with CloudFront, AWS Amplify, Cognito, DynamoDB on-demand).  \\\\n• Make the DEP deterministic—avoid manual console steps unless absolutely unavoidable; call out any nondeterministic IDs and how later steps capture them.  \\\\n\\\\nReturn the full DEP in your report for sequence ID arch_plan_1.\\\"\\n}\\n###arch_plan_1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###arch_plan_1###\\nSYSTEM CALL  \\nType: REPORT  \\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**Deterministically-Executable Plan (DEP)** – HOA Website AWS Foundation\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\nGLOBAL NAMING & VARIABLES (deterministic)\\\\n========================================================\\\\nPROJECT=\\\\\\\"hoa-site\\\\\\\"                     # <= change ONLY this if you must re-namespace\\\\nREGION=\\\\\\\"us-east-1\\\\\\\"                    # keeping everything in one Region for simplicity/cost\\\\nPROFILE=\\\\\\\"default\\\\\\\"                     # AWS CLI profile\\\\n\\\\n# Deterministic resource names (all lower-case, DNS compliant)\\\\nSITE_BUCKET=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-web-bucket\\\\\\\"          # static site origin\\\\nDOCS_BUCKET=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-docs-bucket\\\\\\\"         # private docs\\\\nLOG_BUCKET=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-logs-bucket\\\\\\\"          # access logs\\\\nCOGNITO_POOL_NAME=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-user-pool\\\\\\\"\\\\nCOGNITO_APP_NAME=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-app-client\\\\\\\"\\\\nDDB_TABLE=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-news\\\\\\\"                   # DynamoDB\\\\nLAMBDA_ROLE=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-lambda-role\\\\\\\"\\\\nLAMBDA_FUNC=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-api-function\\\\\\\"\\\\nAPI_NAME=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-http-api\\\\\\\"\\\\nPARAM_PREFIX=\\\\\\\"/hoa-site\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\n1. HIGH-LEVEL ARCHITECTURE (with justifications)\\\\n========================================================\\\\nHosting           : S3 static website + CloudFront (HTTPS, caching, global edge, very low cost)\\\\nAuth              : Amazon Cognito User Pool (simple, secure, fully-managed; Hosted UI removes need for custom auth code)\\\\nProtected Docs    : Private S3 bucket (DOCS_BUCKET). CloudFront serves docs through **Origin Access Control (OAC)**; request is authorized at viewer request time by Lambda@Edge token-validation function (very small Lambda cost, only on protected paths).\\\\nDynamic API       : Amazon API Gateway (HTTP API – cheaper than REST) + AWS Lambda (small, on-demand) + DynamoDB on-demand for structured data (news posts, page metadata). API secured with Cognito JWT Authorizer.\\\\nAdmin UX          : Admin SPA route inside the same React/Vite (or similar) single-page front-end. Non-technical board members log in with Cognito; admin UI calls API to create/update news items (no-code for infra, low-code for content editing).\\\\nCI/CD             : AWS Amplify Console (connect to GitHub). Free 1 000 build minutes/mo; zero admin once set. Amplify handles S3 sync + CloudFront cache-invalidation.\\\\nCost Controls     : S3 & DynamoDB pay-per-use; Lambda 1 M requests & 400k GB-sec free; HTTP API 1 M requests free; CloudFront price-class 100; everything within Free-Tier for small HOA traffic.\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\n2. STEP-BY-STEP AWS CLI / CloudFormation CREATION COMMANDS\\\\n========================================================\\\\nNOTE: All UUID-type outputs (CF Dist ID, Cognito IDs, etc.) are persisted to **SSM Parameter Store** under $PARAM_PREFIX for deterministic retrieval by later teams.\\\\n\\\\nStep 0 – enable helpful CLI features\\\\n```bash\\\\naws configure set default.region $REGION --profile $PROFILE\\\\naws configure set cli_follow_urlparam false --profile $PROFILE\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nStep 1 – S3 buckets (site, docs, logs)\\\\n```bash\\\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket $SITE_BUCKET --acl private --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=$REGION --profile $PROFILE\\\\naws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket $SITE_BUCKET --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\\\\naws s3api put-bucket-website --bucket $SITE_BUCKET --website-configuration '{\\\\\\\"IndexDocument\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"Suffix\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"index.html\\\\\\\"}}'\\\\n\\\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket $DOCS_BUCKET --acl private --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=$REGION\\\\naws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket $DOCS_BUCKET --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\\\\n\\\\naws s3api create-bucket --bucket $LOG_BUCKET --acl log-delivery-write --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint=$REGION\\\\n```\\\\nRecord bucket names (deterministic) to SSM (still helpful for later IaC):\\\\n```bash\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/site-bucket\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$SITE_BUCKET\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/docs-bucket\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$DOCS_BUCKET\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nStep 2 – CloudFront OAC & Distribution\\\\n```bash\\\\n# 2a. Create Origin Access Control (preferred over OAI)\\\\nOAC_ID=$(aws cloudfront create-origin-access-control --origin-access-control-config \\\\\\\\\\\\n          Name=${PROJECT}-oac,Description=\\\\\\\"S3Origin\\\\\\\",SigningProtocol=\\\\\\\"sigv4\\\\\\\",SigningBehavior=\\\\\\\"always\\\\\\\",OriginAccessControlOriginType=\\\\\\\"s3\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\n          --query 'OriginAccessControl.Id' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/oac-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$OAC_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n\\\\n# 2b. Build minimal distribution config JSON deterministically\\\\ncat >cf-config.json <<EOF\\\\n{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"CallerReference\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"$(date +%s)\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Comment\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"${PROJECT} distribution\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Enabled\\\\\\\": true,\\\\n  \\\\\\\"PriceClass\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"PriceClass_100\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Origins\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [\\\\n      {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"siteOrigin\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"DomainName\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"${SITE_BUCKET}.s3.${REGION}.amazonaws.com\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"S3OriginConfig\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"OriginAccessIdentity\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"},\\\\n        \\\\\\\"OriginAccessControlId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"${OAC_ID}\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"docsOrigin\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"DomainName\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"${DOCS_BUCKET}.s3.${REGION}.amazonaws.com\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"S3OriginConfig\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"OriginAccessIdentity\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"},\\\\n        \\\\\\\"OriginAccessControlId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"${OAC_ID}\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    ],\\\\n    \\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 2\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"DefaultCacheBehavior\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"TargetOriginId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"siteOrigin\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"ViewerProtocolPolicy\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"redirect-to-https\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"AllowedMethods\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 2, \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"GET\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"HEAD\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"CachedMethods\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 2, \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"GET\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"HEAD\\\\\\\"]}},\\\\n    \\\\\\\"Compress\\\\\\\": true,\\\\n    \\\\\\\"CachePolicyId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"658327ea-f89d-4fab-a63d-7e88639e58f6\\\\\\\"  # CachingOptimized\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"CacheBehaviors\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 1,\\\\n    \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [\\\\n      {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"PathPattern\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"/protected/*\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"TargetOriginId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"docsOrigin\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"ViewerProtocolPolicy\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"redirect-to-https\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"AllowedMethods\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 2, \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"GET\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"HEAD\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"CachedMethods\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 2, \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"GET\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"HEAD\\\\\\\"]}},\\\\n        \\\\\\\"LambdaFunctionAssociations\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Quantity\\\\\\\": 1,\\\\n          \\\\\\\"Items\\\\\\\": [{\\\\\\\"EventType\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"viewer-request\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"LambdaFunctionARN\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"REPLACE_WITH_AUTH_LAMBDA_ARN\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"IncludeBody\\\\\\\": false}]},\\\\n        \\\\\\\"Compress\\\\\\\": true,\\\\n        \\\\\\\"CachePolicyId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"658327ea-f89d-4fab-a63d-7e88639e58f6\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    ]\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"ViewerCertificate\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"CloudFrontDefaultCertificate\\\\\\\": true},\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Logging\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Bucket\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"${LOG_BUCKET}.s3.amazonaws.com\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"Enabled\\\\\\\": true, \\\\\\\"IncludeCookies\\\\\\\": false, \\\\\\\"Prefix\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"clf/\\\\\\\"}\\\\n}\\\\nEOF\\\\n\\\\nDIST_ID=$(aws cloudfront create-distribution --distribution-config file://cf-config.json --query 'Distribution.Id' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/cf-dist-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$DIST_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n```\\\\n(If a custom domain is later required, ACM + updated ViewerCertificate can be executed deterministically at that time.)\\\\n\\\\nStep 3 – Amazon Cognito User Pool & App Client\\\\n```bash\\\\nUSER_POOL_ID=$(aws cognito-idp create-user-pool --pool-name $COGNITO_POOL_NAME \\\\\\\\\\\\n                 --policies '{\\\\\\\"PasswordPolicy\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"MinimumLength\\\\\\\":8}}' \\\\\\\\\\\\n                 --username-attributes email --auto-verified-attributes email \\\\\\\\\\\\n                 --query 'UserPool.Id' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/user-pool-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$USER_POOL_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n\\\\nAPP_CLIENT_ID=$(aws cognito-idp create-user-pool-client --user-pool-id $USER_POOL_ID --client-name $COGNITO_APP_NAME \\\\\\\\\\\\n                  --generate-secret --allowed-o-auth-flows code --allowed-o-auth-scopes email openid profile \\\\\\\\\\\\n                  --callback-urls \\\\\\\"https://d${DIST_ID}.cloudfront.net/admin/callback\\\\\\\" --supported-identity-providers COGNITO \\\\\\\\\\\\n                  --query 'UserPoolClient.ClientId' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/app-client-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$APP_CLIENT_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nStep 4 – DynamoDB (news posts)\\\\n```bash\\\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name $DDB_TABLE \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --attribute-definitions AttributeName=id,AttributeType=S \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --key-schema AttributeName=id,KeyType=HASH \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/ddb-table\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$DDB_TABLE\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nStep 5 – IAM Role & Lambda skeleton (API + Edge auth)\\\\n```bash\\\\naws iam create-role --role-name $LAMBDA_ROLE \\\\\\\\\\\\n  --assume-role-policy-document file://<(cat <<POLICY\\\\n{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Version\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"2012-10-17\\\\\\\",\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Statement\\\\\\\": [{\\\\\\\"Effect\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Allow\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"Principal\\\\\\\": {\\\\\\\"Service\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"lambda.amazonaws.com\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"edgelambda.amazonaws.com\\\\\\\"]},\\\\\\\"Action\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"sts:AssumeRole\\\\\\\"}]\\\\n}\\\\nPOLICY)\\\\n\\\\naws iam attach-role-policy --role-name $LAMBDA_ROLE --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole\\\\naws iam attach-role-policy --role-name $LAMBDA_ROLE --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess   # narrow later\\\\n\\\\nzip empty.zip -j /dev/null   # placeholder package\\\\nLAMBDA_ARN=$(aws lambda create-function --function-name $LAMBDA_FUNC --runtime nodejs18.x --handler index.handler \\\\\\\\\\\\n              --role arn:aws:iam::$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text):role/$LAMBDA_ROLE \\\\\\\\\\\\n              --zip-file fileb://empty.zip --query 'FunctionArn' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/lambda-api-arn\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$LAMBDA_ARN\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nStep 6 – API Gateway HTTP API integrated with Lambda & Cognito authorizer\\\\n```bash\\\\nAPI_ID=$(aws apigatewayv2 create-api --name $API_NAME --protocol-type HTTP \\\\\\\\\\\\n            --target $LAMBDA_ARN --query 'ApiId' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/http-api-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$API_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n\\\\nAUTH_ID=$(aws apigatewayv2 create-authorizer --api-id $API_ID --authorizer-type JWT \\\\\\\\\\\\n            --identity-source \\\\\\\"$request.header.Authorization\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\n            --jwt-configuration Audience=[$APP_CLIENT_ID],Issuer=https://cognito-idp.$REGION.amazonaws.com/$USER_POOL_ID \\\\\\\\\\\\n            --name CognitoAuth --query 'AuthorizerId' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/api-authorizer-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$AUTH_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nStep 7 – Amplify Console (connects repo; manual token omitted for security)\\\\n```bash\\\\naws amplify create-app --name ${PROJECT}-amplify --repository https://github.com/ORG/REPO \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --platform WEB --iam-service-role arn:aws:iam::$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text):role/${PROJECT}-amplify-role \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --environment-variables \\\\\\\"[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"VITE_API_URL\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"value\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://${API_ID}.execute-api.${REGION}.amazonaws.com\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}]\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --oauth-token GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN_PLACEHOLDER\\\\n# (Amplify will automatically deploy to the SITE_BUCKET & invalidate CloudFront.)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\n3. IAM / SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS\\\\n========================================================\\\\n•  All S3 buckets block public access; CloudFront OAC enforces private-origin access.\\\\n•  Separate bucket for logs with log-delivery-write ACL only.\\\\n•  Lambda role uses **least privilege**; above we attached AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess temporarily—Backend Director must scope to CRUD on $DDB_TABLE only.\\\\n•  Cognito MFA can be enabled later (optional for HOA, trade-off simplicity vs. security).\\\\n•  Parameter Store used to share IDs safely (no code commit of ARNs).\\\\n•  CloudFront signed-cookies OR Lambda@Edge verifies Cognito JWT for /protected/* path, blocking unauthenticated downloads.\\\\n•  Cost limits:\\\\n   – DynamoDB PAY_PER_REQUEST avoids idle capacity charges.\\\\n   – CloudFront PriceClass_100 & logging helps monitor.\\\\n   – Budgets/alerts (out of scope for infra DEP but can be added via AWS Budgets CLI).\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\n4. WORK ITEMS TO DELEGATE\\\\n========================================================\\\\nFrontend Director\\\\n• Build SPA (React/Vite or similar) with public pages + /login (Cognito Hosted UI redirect) + /admin dashboard.\\\\n• Consume VITE_API_URL env var; implement Amplify buildspec.yml.\\\\n• Style and UX.\\\\n\\\\nBackend Director\\\\n• Author Lambda handler code (API) – CRUD news, generate presigned S3 URLs for uploads, admin authorization via Cognito groups.\\\\n• Author Lambda@Edge viewer-request function to validate Cognito token on protected docs.\\\\n• Tighten IAM role policy.\\\\n• Create CloudFormation/ SAM templates to update Lambda & API routes deterministically.\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\nNON-DETERMINISTIC OUTPUT HANDLING SUMMARY\\\\n========================================================\\\\n•  $DIST_ID, $USER_POOL_ID, $APP_CLIENT_ID, $OAC_ID, $API_ID, $AUTH_ID, $LAMBDA_ARN are captured into SSM Parameter Store (names under $PARAM_PREFIX/*). All subsequent scripts resolve them via `aws ssm get-parameter` ensuring determinism.\\\\n\\\\n--- End of DEP ---\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"arch_plan_1\\\"\\n}\\n###arch_plan_1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###arch_rev_1###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (1)\\nTo: 4\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (1) to R (4) with sequence ID arch_rev_1:\\\\n\\\\n]Thank you for a strong initial DEP.  We need a short revision to reconcile with the Frontend & Backend plans:\\\\n\\\\n1. API LAYER\\\\n   • Adopt AWS AppSync GraphQL instead of HTTP API + Lambda for \\\\\\\"News / Documents / Contact\\\\\\\" data because Frontend DEP is built around Amplify Studio Content Manager + DataStore (GraphQL).  \\\\n   • Keep Lambda@Edge viewer-request token validation for /protected/* CloudFront path as you proposed.  \\\\n   • Remove API Gateway/Lambda sections; instead outline deterministic steps to create an AppSync API backed by DynamoDB tables (NewsPosts, ContactMessages).  Use SSM Parameter Store for resulting AppSync API ID & env variables that Frontend/Backend will consume.  \\\\n\\\\n2. RESOURCE NAMING\\\\n   • Retain the deterministic bucket & user-pool names you already defined.  Confirm they are the single source-of-truth so Backend will reference them (no date suffix).  \\\\n\\\\n3. DELEGATED ITEMS\\\\n   • Clarify that Backend Director will supply AppSync resolvers & schema, tighten IAM, and SES e-mail Lambda if we still need server logic for Contact submissions.\\\\n\\\\nDeliver a concise addendum (only the delta) so we keep the main DEP intact.  Return in report for sequence arch_rev_1.\\\"\\n}\\n###arch_rev_1###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###arch_rev_1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"**ADDENDUM — DEP Delta Updates (AppSync & Delegations)**\\\\n(Only changes; everything not mentioned remains as in original DEP.)\\\\n\\\\n========================================================\\\\n1. API LAYER – Replace API Gateway + Lambda with AWS AppSync GraphQL\\\\n========================================================\\\\nREMOVE: Step 6 that created API Gateway & authorizer.\\\\nADD NEW STEP 6 (fully deterministic CLI):\\\\n```bash\\\\n# ---------- Step 6 – AppSync GraphQL API backed by DynamoDB ----------\\\\nAPPSYNC_API_NAME=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-graphql-api\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# 6a. Create GraphQL API secured by Cognito\\\\nAPPSYNC_API_ID=$(aws appsync create-graphql-api \\\\\\\\ \\\\n    --name $APPSYNC_API_NAME \\\\\\\\\\\\n    --authentication-type AMAZON_COGNITO_USER_POOLS \\\\\\\\\\\\n    --user-pool-config userPoolId=$(aws ssm get-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/user-pool-id\\\\\\\" --query 'Parameter.Value' --output text),awsRegion=$REGION,defaultAction=ALLOW \\\\\\\\\\\\n    --query 'graphqlApi.apiId' --output text)\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/appsync-api-id\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$APPSYNC_API_ID\\\\\\\" --type String\\\\n\\\\n# 6b. Provision DynamoDB tables (if not already created)\\\\nNEWS_TABLE=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-NewsPosts\\\\\\\"      # deterministic\\\\nCONTACT_TABLE=\\\\\\\"${PROJECT}-ContactMsgs\\\\\\\" # deterministic\\\\n\\\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name $NEWS_TABLE \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --attribute-definitions AttributeName=id,AttributeType=S \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --key-schema AttributeName=id,KeyType=HASH \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST || true   # idempotent if exists\\\\naws dynamodb create-table --table-name $CONTACT_TABLE \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --attribute-definitions AttributeName=id,AttributeType=S \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --key-schema AttributeName=id,KeyType=HASH \\\\\\\\\\\\n     --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST || true\\\\n\\\\n# record table names for backend\\\\naws ssm put-parameter --name \\\\\\\"$PARAM_PREFIX/table-news\\\\\\\" --value \\\\\\\"$NEWS_TABLE\\\\\\\" --type String\